{
 "about": "Shared vectors for how Slate hears numbers and symbols. pytest checks them against slate.timing.spoken and slate.timing.words (tests/story/test_timing_spoken.py); vitest checks them in the engine (engine/tests/story/spoken_vectors.test.ts). Any implementation must reproduce them exactly, in order.",
 "rules": {
  "normalize": "A word's match key: Unicode NFKC, then lower case, then keep only letters, digits and the language table's kept symbols (normalize.keep, '%&$€£¥#@+' in English).",
  "forms": "forms(token) lists the ways a token is said, most usual first. Punctuation around the token is dropped first (spoken symbols and a leading minus stay). A plain word is said as itself; punctuation alone says nothing.",
  "match": "find(words, query, occ): scan the heard words left to right. At each word that has a key, try each target (the query's own key first, then each spoken form's keys joined) against the joined keys of 1 to min(max_run, form length + 2) heard words and take the longest run that equals one. Failing that, a heard word that isn't all letters (a number or a symbol) matches on its own when one of its targets is the query's own key ('12' for 'twelve', '%' for 'percent'). Each hit counts one occurrence and skips past its run; span is [first, last+1) of the occ-th hit, or null. targets and heard_targets list the match keys, for implementations that don't compute spoken forms themselves.",
  "max_run": 6
 },
